Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
in recent years we have made important  progress toward the final conquest of  tuberculosis to a great extent aided by  newer and better hospitals and  sanatorius and naturally as every health  officer knows such a hospital or  sanatorium is the best place to treat  tuberculosis when it is possible  with our elaborate equipment constant  and thorough supervision and complete  facilities these hospitals help patients  to conquer their disease  they give more effective help because  they can apply definite principles of  isolation treatment education and  rehabilitation  every nurse has had some glimpse of  these principles at work during her  professional training and experience the  first element of tuberculosis care is  the understanding between doctor and  nurse this understanding is vital to  successful collaboration in the care of  the patient  it is also vital to the relationship of  the nurse to the patient and family  always the doctor is responsible for  making the diagnosis the doctor also  orders medication and plans treatment  the nurse is responsible for  administering the doctor's plan she  shares with the doctor responsibility  for teaching patients and family  seldom can be equal in any home the  effectiveness of hospital care but we  can apply a number of the same definite  principles which are familiar elements  in contagious disease nursing technique  in the scenes which follow you'll see a  typical family in a typical case for the  public health nursing compared to  hospital nursing there are both  similarities and differences in this  case from the time the doctor in charge  calls upon the public health nurse miss  abbott this is dr cannell i have another  patient for you  the name is evan  he has a wife  and has two children  the involvement  is bilateral  the sputum is positive  the family will need your usual  instruction  will you meet me there at 11 please 11  o'clock  yes  yes i know  i'll see you there doctor  like the nurse in the hospital this  public health nurse will follow the  doctor's orders for treatment but she  must also pass on the responsibility for  daily care to the family following the  doctor's orders she will help him to  explain the treatment of the family and  will teach some responsible member to  follow out the routine of care required  for the patient's welfare and for their  own safety you will see brief examples  of teaching by this nurse keep in mind  as you watch them that she has carried  over to public health service from her  hospital training  five proven elements of teaching which  you will see are used  here they are  principles of teaching that are applied  in all effective education first  good hospital teaching is affirmative  the teacher  must make far less use of don't  than of do  second  the item to be taught must be stated  clearly in a few simple words  keep instruction short  third  the teacher must demonstrate what she  has described  fourth  the learner must demonstrate that he  fully understands  the instructions  finally use printed material  as a reminder of your teaching get it  from your health department or  tuberculosis association  material can never take the place of  person-to-person teaching any more than  a short motion picture can teach  complete home nursing but it can help  make your teaching more effective by  crystallizing the details of treatment  but when it comes to such a problem as  the patient's morale printed material is  of little use that factor must be  governed only by the human approach  this element of morale is so important  that even before teaching this family  the routine of care and before showing  the patient how to protect his family  the nurse tries to establish the right  attitude in the patient's mind she  chooses her words and her approach  carefully explaining simply trying to  install the will to recover she makes it  clear that treatment is not just  something we do to the patient but  something to which he must contribute  the patient's morale is one of the  determining factors in his recovery and  it controls the extent of the protection  he gives the family with the  understanding and cooperation of the  patient the nurse's work will be more  successful she must make him believe it  is worthwhile to get well  the next treatment principle to be  transferred from hospital to home is  separation of the patient from the  community  this can best be done when we remove the  patient to a tuberculosis hospital or  sanatorium  in a well-conducted hospital  separation from the community is  combined with evidence that treatment is  conducted for the patient's own benefit  and protection  in the home we must apply whatever we  can of communicable disease nurturing  techniques when possible we try to  assign the patient to a single private  room in any case a separate bed is  essential and the patient must have  completely separate equipment as well  dishes linens and personal needs should  be kept apart the nurse explains this  affirmatively as routine medical  procedure without over emphasizing the  isolation of the patient she persuades  patient and family that separation will  protect the patient as well as protect  others from his bacilli she keeps a  safety zone as wide as possible  now she improvises visible barriers to  symbolize the privacy of her patient  even in a one-room dwelling naturally it  is important to protect others from the  tuberculosis bacilli which invades every  corner of the room these paratroopers  can carry tuberculosis from patient to  family  but their near relatives carry coals and  pneumonia from family to patient too  so a relative's cold can complicate your  patient's illness dangerously  after this is explained clearly to the  patient and family the nurse leaves this  printed reminder the public health nurse  has been hailed as a shoe leather  epidemiologist her teaching about  infection provides a natural  introduction to this part of her service  checking the patient's contacts here is  a positive method of identifying all  contacts which has helped to find a lot  of tuberculosis  there  represented by the square you have the  patient  a circle for the white the line of  descent and the two children  shade the square for a known active case  put a question mark for each known  contact  does a patient have any brothers or  sisters  show them  and here's your carrier a sister who  died of tuberculosis  the system is simple  modify the basic idea and it'll fit any  family condition  it's an effective aid in finding cases  early enough to make treatment  successful  and now we come to our next factor of  treatment  cleanliness  simple cleanliness helps to fight  tuberculosis  we cannot duplicate in the home complete  hospital apparatus  but we can teach the value and  importance of cleanliness  case is so primitive that soap and water  cannot be used as a supplement to soap  and water explain the cleansing  properties of sunlight affirmatively to  the family  teach the family that cleanliness helps  to protect everyone that they as well as  the patient guard themselves by  practicing cleanliness make the family  understand how cleanliness particularly  with dishes and other objects used by  the patients is a fundamental necessity  and teach the patient too that his  cleanliness will protect the family he  must learn to do his part to prevent  further infection of himself and others  the use of disposable tissues must be  made perfectly clear to the patient the  whole family must understand how to  handle such contaminated material  this type of instruction shows very  plainly the steps in effective teaching  as they apply in the home care of  tuberculosis  watch now the application of these  elements as we explain the disposal of  contaminated tissues to the family  first  explanation  then  demonstration show the whole procedure  then you get participation by having a  procedure carried out for you  now explanation demonstration and  participation are repeated in stressing  a second point in the procedure that the  family must guard themselves to by  constant cleanliness here shown by  washing the hands thoroughly after  handling contaminated material  and finally now the printed reminder  covers both points in the procedure  we'll purposely delay the vital fifth  principle of treatment which is rest but  we need a foundation upon which rest  will be possible and effective  notice how the principles of treatment  form a pyramid  confidence in medical direction is  essential in all treatment  rest is a little more than a word until  we establish the opportunity of complete  separation  and privacy  a clean bed and a clean body  are great helps in relaxation and rest  remember  the family has never seen bed rest in a  tuberculosis hospital  you must explain it to them  bed rest means being washed and bathed  and shaved in bed  bed rest means too  being fed in bed not getting up for any  reason  and most important bed rest means a  recumbent position not sitting up until  the doctor prescribes that chain  finally bed rest means regular daily  intervals of complete relaxation rest  for both body and mind  the patient may shorten his illness by  giving hours each day to absolute bed  rest  and it is necessary to explain gently  firmly to the family and to friends that  listening to others is not rest  the next important item is nutrition not  only what to eat but the balance of  meals  talk in terms of a common sense menu  which can be purchased by the patient's  family with the funds available  the question of nutrition may well be  taken up in the instruction of the  second visit in a fresh sterile gown the  nurse teaches the family to avoid any  contamination of the food supply  in planning a diet assure the family  that the patient makes little real gain  by being stuck and that weight alone  does not mean recovery  show the family how to improvise a tray  and how to make the food on that tray  look attractive  the result will help to stimulate the  patient's appetite and his intake of the  right foods for his needs  yes even a meal that's good for you can  be appetizing  suitable recreation when medically  approved is an important factor in  inducing patients to accept treatment  whether in the hospital or in the home  your instruction to the family is not  complete  unless you yourself  have given some thought  and  provoke in the home some thought as to  what the patient may do for recreation  during his illness  especially when he becomes sputum  negative  now here is an example of hand work  learned from an occupational therapist  and talk to the patient under the  doctor's approval  as the patient continues to make  progress his reading time may be  gradually increased and now with his  recovery approaching the public health  nurse arranges for a conference doctor i  came to see if the patient is ready for  rehabilitation training mr bronson is  from the state agency yes he should be  ready to start on mr bronson's plan the  first of next month  figuring on three months training can we  put him on a job when it's completed  i believe we can count on that send him  in for a recheck regularly while he is  training  if all continues to go well the physical  demands you describe here will be well  within its tolerance thank you  yes here is our ultimate goal the final  reward for patient effort care and  thought  to get the patient back on his feet back  to a job suitable to his condition and  the future needs of his family to make  him a working contributing member of the  community  you may obtain printed material for the  instruction the patient of the family  from the agency that arranged the  showing of this film  make yourself familiar with all the  publications available from your local  tuberculosis association  you

